    <div class="outcome-card" id="outcome-card">
      <div class="outcome-label">🎯 Your outcome</div>
      <p class="outcome-text">By the end of this module, you will have built a working Lunar Lander simulator that you can play, test automatically, and extend with an autopilot. Every Python concept is introduced the moment the game needs it.</p>
      <div class="outcome-grid">
        <div class="outcome-item"><span class="oi-icon">📦</span><span>Variables & constants</span></div>
        <div class="outcome-item"><span class="oi-icon">📖</span><span>Dictionaries</span></div>
        <div class="outcome-item"><span class="oi-icon">⚙️</span><span>Functions</span></div>
        <div class="outcome-item"><span class="oi-icon">🔁</span><span>Loops</span></div>
        <div class="outcome-item"><span class="oi-icon">🔀</span><span>Conditionals</span></div>
        <div class="outcome-item"><span class="oi-icon">🧪</span><span>Automated tests</span></div>
        <div class="outcome-item"><span class="oi-icon">📊</span><span>Matplotlib</span></div>
        <div class="outcome-item"><span class="oi-icon">🤖</span><span>Autopilot challenge</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- History callout -->
    <div class="history-card">
      <div class="history-inner">
        <div class="history-year">1969</div>
        <div class="history-text">
          <strong>The original Lunar Lander</strong> was written by David Ahl on a DEC PDP-8 in FOCAL — a computer with 4KB of RAM. The units were Imperial: feet, feet per second, pounds of fuel. We're keeping the Imperial units. It feels right.
          <br><br>
          The Apollo Guidance Computer that actually landed humans on the Moon ran at <strong>2 MHz with 4KB of RAM</strong>. It solved real-time trajectory control. You'll be doing the same — just with slightly more memory.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Steps container -->
    <div class="steps" id="steps-container">

      <!-- Step 1 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-1">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(1)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">01</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Constants — The Rules of the Universe</div>
              <div class="step-concept">Variables · Naming conventions</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-1">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-1">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> The computer has no memory of its own. You have to tell it everything — and give each thing a name so you can refer to it later. Constants are values that never change: gravity doesn't change, the safe landing speed doesn't change.
          </div>
          <p>We write constants in <code>UPPER_CASE</code> by convention so we remember they're fixed.</p>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code># The physical constants of our simulation
# (Imperial units — just like the 1969 original)

G          = 5.0    # Lunar gravity, ft/s²
MAX_ACCEL  = 15.0   # Max upward accel at 100% thrust, ft/s²
FUEL_RATE  = 1.0    # Fuel consumed per second at 100% thrust
DT         = 0.5    # Time step — seconds per tick
SAFE_SPEED = 7.0    # Maximum safe landing speed, ft/s

# Starting conditions
START_ALT  = 2500.0  # Starting altitude, ft
START_VEL  = -450.0  # Starting velocity (negative = falling)
START_FUEL = 600.0   # Starting fuel, lb

print(f"Starting altitude: {START_ALT} ft")
print(f"Starting velocity: {START_VEL} ft/s")
print(f"Safe landing speed: ≤{SAFE_SPEED} ft/s")</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> Change <code>START_ALT</code> to <code>500</code> and re-run. What changes in the output? Change it back when you're done.
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 Variables are named containers for values. Constants are variables we promise never to change.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(1)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 2 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-2">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(2)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">02</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">State — Tracking the Lander</div>
              <div class="step-concept">Dictionaries · Functions · Docstrings</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-2">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-2">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> The lander has a <em>state</em> — a snapshot of where it is and what it's doing right now. We could use four separate variables, but that gets messy fast. A dictionary keeps everything together as one thing.
          </div>
          <p>Our lander state has four values: <code>altitude</code>, <code>velocity</code>, <code>fuel</code>, and <code>thrust_pct</code>.</p>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code>def initial_state():
    """Return a fresh lander state dictionary."""
    return {
        'time':       0.0,
        'altitude':   START_ALT,
        'velocity':   START_VEL,
        'fuel':       START_FUEL,
        'thrust_pct': 0,
    }

# Test it
lander = initial_state()
print("Initial lander state:")
for key, value in lander.items():
    print(f"  {key:12s} = {value}")</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> Access a single value with <code>print(lander['altitude'])</code>. Try changing a value: <code>lander['thrust_pct'] = 50</code>. Print the whole dict again — did it change?
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 Dictionaries store named values. Functions (<code>def</code>) let you reuse code. The <code>"""docstring"""</code> explains what a function does.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(2)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 3 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-3">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(3)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">03</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Physics — Gravity and Thrust</div>
              <div class="step-concept">Arithmetic · Functions · Return values</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-3">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-3">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> This is the heart of the simulation. Every 0.5 seconds, we calculate what happens to the lander based on the laws of physics. Thrust pushes up. Gravity pulls down. The net result changes velocity. Velocity changes altitude.
          </div>
          <div class="physics-box">
            <div class="physics-eq">acceleration = (thrust% ÷ 100) × MAX_ACCEL − G</div>
            <div class="physics-eq">velocity = velocity + acceleration × DT</div>
            <div class="physics-eq">altitude = altitude + velocity × DT</div>
            <div class="physics-eq">fuel = fuel − (thrust% ÷ 100) × FUEL_RATE × DT</div>
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code>def step(state):
    """Advance the simulation by one time step (DT seconds)."""
    thrust = max(0, min(100, state['thrust_pct'])) / 100.0

    acceleration = thrust * MAX_ACCEL - G
    velocity     = state['velocity'] + acceleration * DT
    altitude     = state['altitude'] + velocity * DT
    fuel         = max(0.0, state['fuel'] - thrust * FUEL_RATE * DT)

    return {
        'time':       state['time'] + DT,
        'altitude':   altitude,
        'velocity':   velocity,
        'fuel':       fuel,
        'thrust_pct': state['thrust_pct'],
    }

# Test: no thrust — should fall
s = initial_state()
for _ in range(4):
    s = step(s)
    print(f"alt={s['altitude']:.1f}ft  vel={s['velocity']:.1f}ft/s")</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> Set <code>s['thrust_pct'] = 100</code> before the loop and re-run. What happens to velocity? Does it stop falling?
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 Functions return new data. We don't modify state <em>in place</em> — we return a new copy. This makes bugs easier to find.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(3)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 4 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-4">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(4)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">04</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Display — The HUD</div>
              <div class="step-concept">F-strings · String formatting</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-4">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-4">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> Numbers are useless if you can't read them. A Heads-Up Display shows the player what's happening each tick. We need numbers aligned, labelled, and readable at a glance.
          </div>
          <div class="hud-example">t= 12.5s | alt= 1843.2 ft | vel=  -387.1 ft/s | fuel= 548 lb | thrust= 75%</div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code>def hud(state):
    """Return a formatted HUD string for the current state."""
    return (
        f"t={state['time']:5.1f}s | "
        f"alt={state['altitude']:7.1f} ft | "
        f"vel={state['velocity']:8.1f} ft/s | "
        f"fuel={state['fuel']:4.0f} lb | "
        f"thrust={state['thrust_pct']:3d}%"
    )

# Test it
s = initial_state()
print(hud(s))
s['thrust_pct'] = 75
s = step(s)
print(hud(s))</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> The format spec <code>:7.1f</code> means 7 characters wide, 1 decimal place. Change <code>:7.1f</code> to <code>:10.2f</code>. What changes?
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 F-strings embed expressions directly in text. Format specs (<code>:7.1f</code>) control width and precision.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(4)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 5 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-5">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(5)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">05</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">The Game Loop — Making it Playable</div>
              <div class="step-concept">While loops · Input · Conditionals · Break</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-5">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-5">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> A game loop runs continuously: show state → get input → update → check if over → repeat. The <code>while</code> loop keeps going until we tell it to stop. The game is over when we land (or crash).
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code>def is_landed(state):
    return state['altitude'] <= 0

def is_safe(state):
    return is_landed(state) and abs(state['velocity']) <= SAFE_SPEED

def play():
    state = initial_state()
    print("🚀 LUNAR LANDER — enter thrust 0-100 each tick. 'q' to quit.")
    print(f"Goal: land with speed ≤ {SAFE_SPEED} ft/s")
    print("-" * 65)

    while True:
        print(hud(state))

        if is_landed(state):
            if is_safe(state):
                print(f"✅ SAFE LANDING! Speed: {abs(state['velocity']):.1f} ft/s")
            else:
                print(f"💥 CRASH! Impact speed: {abs(state['velocity']):.1f} ft/s")
            break

        cmd = input("thrust% > ").strip().lower()
        if cmd == 'q':
            break
        if cmd.isdigit():
            state['thrust_pct'] = max(0, min(100, int(cmd)))

        state = step(state)

play()</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Play it.</strong> Try a few runs. Try 0% all the way down. Try 100%. Find a strategy that works. Hint: you need to burn hard early to slow your initial 450 ft/s fall, then ease off, then burn again for final approach.
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 <code>while True</code> loops forever until <code>break</code>. <code>input()</code> pauses execution and waits for the user. <code>abs()</code> gives absolute value.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(5)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 6 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-6">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(6)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">06</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Replay & Analysis — Visualising Runs</div>
              <div class="step-concept">Lists · Matplotlib · Functions as arguments</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-6">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-6">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> Playing manually is fun, but we want to compare strategies. If we record every state, we can plot the full trajectory — altitude, velocity, and fuel over time.
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code">import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

def simulate(thrust_schedule):
    """Run a simulation from a thrust schedule (list of (time, thrust) tuples)."""
    state = initial_state()
    history = [dict(state)]
    schedule_dict = dict(thrust_schedule)

    for _ in range(int(600 / DT)):
        t = state['time']
        candidates = [v for k, v in schedule_dict.items() if k <= t]
        state['thrust_pct'] = candidates[-1] if candidates else 0
        state = step(state)
        history.append(dict(state))
        if is_landed(state):
            break
    return history

# Try a manual schedule
run = simulate([
    (0,   80),   # start: burn hard
    (30,  40),   # ease off
    (80,  70),   # ramp up for approach
    (120, 90),   # heavy burn for landing
])

# Plot it
times = [s['time']     for s in run]
alts  = [s['altitude'] for s in run]
vels  = [s['velocity'] for s in run]

fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(10, 6), sharex=True)
ax1.plot(times, alts, color='steelblue')
ax1.set_ylabel('Altitude (ft)'); ax1.grid(True, alpha=0.3)
ax2.plot(times, vels, color='tomato')
ax2.axhline(-SAFE_SPEED, color='green', linestyle='--', label='Safe speed')
ax2.set_ylabel('Velocity (ft/s)'); ax2.set_xlabel('Time (s)')
ax2.legend(); ax2.grid(True, alpha=0.3)
plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> Change the thrust schedule values. What does 90% at t=0 do to the altitude curve? What if you wait too long to burn?
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 Lists of tuples. List comprehensions (<code>[s['time'] for s in run]</code>). Matplotlib subplots.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(6)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 7 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-7">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(7)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">07</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Automated Tests — Verifying Your Physics</div>
              <div class="step-concept">Assert statements · Test functions · TDD thinking</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-7">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-7">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> How do you know your physics is correct? You could play the game and check — or you could write code that checks it for you. Automated tests run in milliseconds and never lie.
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code">def run_ticks(thrust_pct, ticks=10):
    s = initial_state()
    s['thrust_pct'] = thrust_pct
    for _ in range(ticks):
        s = step(s)
    return s

def test_simulation_advances():
    s = run_ticks(thrust_pct=0, ticks=4)
    assert s['time'] > 0, "Time should increase"
    assert s['altitude'] != START_ALT, "Altitude should change"
    print("✅ test_simulation_advances passed")

def test_gravity_causes_descent():
    s = run_ticks(thrust_pct=0, ticks=6)
    assert s['velocity'] < START_VEL, "Should fall faster"
    assert s['altitude'] < START_ALT, "Should be lower"
    print("✅ test_gravity_causes_descent passed")

def test_thrust_counters_gravity():
    s0 = run_ticks(thrust_pct=0,   ticks=8)
    s1 = run_ticks(thrust_pct=100, ticks=8)
    assert s1['velocity'] > s0['velocity'], "Thrust should help"
    print("✅ test_thrust_counters_gravity passed")

# Run all
test_simulation_advances()
test_gravity_causes_descent()
test_thrust_counters_gravity()</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Break a test on purpose:</strong> Change <code>G = 5.0</code> to <code>G = -5.0</code> at the top and re-run the tests. Which ones fail? Why? Change it back.
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 <code>assert condition, "message"</code> raises an error if the condition is false. Test functions define what <em>must</em> be true about your code.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(7)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 8 -->
      <div class="step-card step-challenge" id="step-8">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(8)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">08</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Autopilot Challenge 🏆</div>
              <div class="step-concept">Functions as arguments · Control logic · Optimisation</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-8">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-8">
          <div class="challenge-banner">This step has no ceiling. Start simple — go as far as you want.</div>
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>The challenge:</strong> Write a function that lands the lander <em>automatically</em>, using as little fuel as possible. The function receives the current state and returns a thrust percentage.
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code">def autopilot(state):
    """
    Autopilot controller. Returns thrust percentage (0-100).
    state keys: altitude, velocity, fuel, time, thrust_pct
    """
    alt = state['altitude']
    vel = state['velocity']

    # TODO: replace with something smarter
    if vel < -50:
        return 80
    else:
        return 20

# Run it
run = simulate(autopilot)   # pass the function, not the result
final = run[-1]
print(f"Final speed: {abs(final['velocity']):.1f} ft/s")
print(f"Outcome: {'✅ SAFE' if is_safe(final) else '💥 CRASH'}")</code></pre></div>

          <div class="hints-container">
            <div class="hint-item" id="hint1">
              <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="toggleHint('hint1')">💡 Hint 1 — Think about target velocity</button>
              <div class="hint-body" style="display:none">At any altitude, there's a speed that would bring you to a safe stop at the surface. If you're falling faster than that, burn harder. If you're already slow enough, ease off.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="hint-item" id="hint2">
              <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="toggleHint('hint2')">💡 Hint 2 — Proportional control</button>
              <div class="hint-body" style="display:none">The simplest effective controller: <code>error = target_velocity - actual_velocity</code>. The bigger the error, the harder you burn. Try: <code>thrust = 50 + k * error</code> for some constant <code>k</code>. Clamp the result to 0–100.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="hint-item" id="hint3">
              <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="toggleHint('hint3')">💡 Hint 3 — Target velocity from altitude</button>
              <div class="hint-body" style="display:none">Try: <code>target_vel = -alt / 20</code>. At 2500ft that's -125 ft/s. At 100ft it's -5 ft/s. It naturally gets gentler as you approach the surface. This alone might get you a safe landing.</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="autopilot-tests">
            <div class="at-label">Your autopilot must pass these three tests:</div>
            <div class="code-block"><pre><code>def test_autopilot_lands():
    run = simulate(autopilot)
    assert is_landed(run[-1]), "Must reach the surface"
    print("✅ lands")

def test_autopilot_lands_safely():
    run = simulate(autopilot)
    assert is_safe(run[-1]), f"Too fast: {abs(run[-1]['velocity']):.1f} ft/s"
    print("✅ lands safely")

def test_autopilot_efficient():
    run = simulate(autopilot)
    fuel_used = (START_FUEL - run[-1]['fuel']) / START_FUEL * 100
    assert fuel_used < 80, f"Used {fuel_used:.0f}% of fuel — try to be more efficient"
    print(f"✅ efficient ({fuel_used:.0f}% fuel used)")

test_autopilot_lands()
test_autopilot_lands_safely()
test_autopilot_efficient()</code></pre></div>
          </div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(8)">✓ All tests pass — module complete!</button>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div><!-- /steps -->

    <!-- Completion card -->
    <div class="completion-card" id="completion-card" style="display:none">
      <div class="completion-inner">
        <div class="completion-icon">🌕</div>
        <h2>Module complete.</h2>
